1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
The engine has to burn

2
00:00:01,020 --> 00:00:02,530
for about fifteen seconds.

3
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:04,630
The computer is off.

4
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,000
And three astronauts are trying

5
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:08,620
to steer a damaged spacecraft

6
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,070
back to Earth.

7
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:11,440
Jim Lovell looks out the

8
00:00:11,460 --> 00:00:12,440
window to keep the planet

9
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:13,110
in view.

10
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:14,580
The men have been awake

11
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:16,060
too long, the cabin is

12
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:16,470
cold,

13
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,100
and every remaining bit of

14
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:18,850
power matters.

15
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:21,100
Somewhere on board is a

16
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:22,780
small, old-fashioned answer to

17
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:24,230
one urgent question:

18
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:25,860
when do we shut the

19
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:28,220
engine off? The answer is

20
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,010
a wristwatch.

21
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,030
A mechanical chronograph.

22
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:32,980
Jack Swigert has one on

23
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:34,300
his wrist, and the Science

24
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:36,380
Museum credits his watch with

25
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,600
timing crucial engine firings

26
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:40,330
during Apollo 13.

27
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:41,920
How did a watch end

28
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:42,840
up with a job on

29
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:43,730
a trip to the Moon?

30
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:45,700
And why would NASA trust

31
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:47,460
it when the spacecraft itself

32
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:48,370
was in trouble?

33
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:50,490
In an Apollo photograph,

34
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:52,240
almost everything looks

35
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:53,810
specially built for space.

36
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:55,970
The suit. The helmet.

37
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:57,790
The spacecraft behind it.

38
00:00:58,440 --> 00:00:59,450
Then there is the watch:

39
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:01,240
a dial, a couple of

40
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:01,590
buttons,

41
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:03,040
and a strap over the

42
00:01:03,060 --> 00:01:03,390
sleeve.

43
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:05,570
is an Omega Speedmaster.

44
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:07,680
A chronograph is a stopwatch

45
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:08,430
you can wear.

46
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:10,340
Press one button and a

47
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:11,850
hand starts counting seconds.

48
00:01:12,580 --> 00:01:13,820
Press it again and it

49
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:14,190
stops.

50
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:16,040
That matters when a task

51
00:01:16,060 --> 00:01:17,740
must last a precise number

52
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:18,350
of seconds:

53
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:19,370
an experiment,

54
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:21,640
a maneuver, a stretch outside

55
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:22,450
the spacecraft.

56
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,980
The Speedmaster does not need

57
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,400
the ship's electricity to do

58
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:26,990
that job.

59
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:28,690
You wind it by hand.

60
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:30,380
The watch is not smarter

61
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:31,210
than the spacecraft.

62
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:33,390
It has one simple advantage:

63
00:01:34,080 --> 00:01:35,180
it keeps doing its job

64
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:36,740
when another system has to

65
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:37,530
be switched off.

66
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:39,580
That was useful long before

67
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,610
Apollo 13.

68
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,320
But first the watch had

69
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,900
to persuade NASA that it

70
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:44,950
belonged there at all.

71
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,500
Imagine buying a watch and

72
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:48,900
asking the salesperson a few

73
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:49,410
questions.

74
00:01:50,260 --> 00:01:51,190
Will it survive water?

75
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:52,270
A hard knock?

76
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:53,410
Magnetism?

77
00:01:53,920 --> 00:01:56,080
Now add violent acceleration and

78
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:57,200
temperatures that would make an

79
00:01:57,220 --> 00:01:58,800
ordinary day feel very far

80
00:01:58,820 --> 00:01:59,290
away.

81
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:00,820
That was the sort of

82
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:02,120
list NASA brought to its

83
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:03,790
search for a crew chronograph.

84
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:05,600
NASA tested watches for

85
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:06,770
precision and reliability.

86
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:08,980
The Speedmaster made the cut

87
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,780
and was qualified for crewed

88
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:12,270
spaceflight in 1965.

89
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:13,870
By Apollo,

90
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:15,740
it was mission equipment rather

91
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:17,550
than a lucky personal accessory.

92
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:18,920
The choice had a quiet

93
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:19,610
logic.

94
00:02:20,420 --> 00:02:21,500
A watch could travel with

95
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:22,010
an astronaut,

96
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:23,970
work inside or outside,

97
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:25,520
and measure an interval without

98
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:27,310
asking the spacecraft for power.

99
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,110
You can call that redundancy.

100
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:30,880
In the middle of a

101
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,500
crisis, it feels more like

102
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,010
having one extra chance.

103
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,140
When Apollo 11 landed on

104
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:36,650
the Moon,

105
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:38,440
Neil Armstrong faced a strange

106
00:02:38,460 --> 00:02:39,090
choice:

107
00:02:39,680 --> 00:02:41,760
take his Speedmaster outside, or

108
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:42,480
leave it in the lunar

109
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,010
module.

110
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:44,570
He left it inside.

111
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:46,540
The module's timer had caused

112
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:48,140
trouble, and a watch could

113
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:49,760
serve as a backup while

114
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:51,140
Buzz Aldrin took his own

115
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:52,130
onto the surface.

116
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:53,700
That is how the astronauts

117
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,410
later remembered the decision.

118
00:02:56,380 --> 00:02:57,240
The man to step onto

119
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,400
the Moon left his watch

120
00:02:58,420 --> 00:02:58,850
indoors.

121
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:01,100
The famous photograph was less

122
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:02,740
important than having a way

123
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,060
to keep time inside the

124
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:04,670
lander.

125
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:06,780
The Speedmaster earned its

126
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:07,990
nickname on the Moon.

127
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:09,700
The next chapter would show

128
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:10,870
a different side of it.

129
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:12,580
A mission that never reached

130
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,200
the lunar surface would need

131
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:15,690
every backup it had.

132
00:03:16,480 --> 00:03:17,800
Apollo 13 had a landing

133
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:19,000
site and a crew of

134
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:19,390
three.

135
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:21,310
Jim Lovell was the commander.

136
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,100
Fred Haise was the lunar

137
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:23,890
module pilot.

138
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:25,900
Jack Swigert joined at the

139
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:27,560
last minute, replacing Ken

140
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:29,370
Mattingly after a measles scare.

141
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:31,910
Swigert wore a Speedmaster.

142
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:33,640
His flight watch is now

143
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:34,950
in the Smithsonian collection.

144
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:36,640
It was a small part

145
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:37,720
of the equipment on board

146
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:38,870
and, at first,

147
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,500
hardly the most exciting thing

148
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:41,030
to watch.

149
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,800
For nearly two days, the

150
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:45,100
flight was routine enough that

151
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:46,720
a controller in Houston joked

152
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:47,730
they were bored.

153
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:49,560
Then the crew finished a

154
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:50,550
television broadcast.

155
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:52,920
Nine minutes later, an oxygen

156
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:53,930
tank exploded.

157
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:56,110
A bang shook the spacecraft.

158
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:57,710
Alarms followed.

159
00:03:58,580 --> 00:03:59,880
Within hours, the Moon landing

160
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:00,570
was gone.

161
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:02,260
The new mission was simpler

162
00:04:02,280 --> 00:04:03,820
to say and much harder

163
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:04,390
to do:

164
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:06,390
bring all three men home.

165
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,660
The command module lost its

166
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:10,010
normal supply of electricity,

167
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:11,550
light and water.

168
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:13,640
Imagine your house suddenly

169
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:14,790
losing all three,

170
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:16,660
then remember that this house

171
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:17,930
is moving through space.

172
00:04:18,900 --> 00:04:20,580
crew moved into the lunar

173
00:04:20,600 --> 00:04:21,050
module,

174
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:22,680
a machine built to carry

175
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:23,800
two people down to the

176
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:24,470
Moon and back.

177
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:26,220
Now it had to keep

178
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:27,720
three people alive long enough

179
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:28,810
to return to Earth.

180
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:30,530
At Mission Control,

181
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,340
teams wrote and tested new

182
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:32,890
procedures

183
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:34,620
while the astronauts dealt with

184
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:36,750
a cold cabin, dwindling water

185
00:04:37,240 --> 00:04:38,420
and an air system that

186
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:40,050
needed an improvised fix.

187
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:42,700
The watch cannot clean the

188
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:44,680
air or tell Houston where

189
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:45,790
the spacecraft is.

190
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:47,340
But the rescue is made

191
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:48,730
of hundreds of small jobs.

192
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:50,740
Some require a room full

193
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:51,230
of engineers.

194
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:53,460
Others require someone to count

195
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,280
seconds accurately while the

196
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,050
engines run.

197
00:04:56,940 --> 00:04:58,120
First, the crew had to

198
00:04:58,140 --> 00:04:59,140
get back onto a path

199
00:04:59,160 --> 00:05:00,940
around the Moon and toward

200
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:01,550
Earth.

201
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:03,820
They fired the lunar module's

202
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:04,170
engine.

203
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,880
Later, after swinging past the

204
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:08,500
far side, they fired it

205
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:09,910
again to shorten the journey

206
00:05:10,180 --> 00:05:11,440
and change where they would

207
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:11,830
land.

208
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:14,480
Each maneuver bought them a

209
00:05:14,500 --> 00:05:15,150
better chance.

210
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:16,500
But they still had to

211
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:17,720
arrive at Earth at the

212
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:18,670
right angle.

213
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:20,820
Too shallow and the craft

214
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:21,890
could skip away.

215
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:24,200
Too steep and reentry would

216
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:25,820
become dangerous. Their

217
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,360
remaining power and water were

218
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:29,720
too precious to use the

219
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:31,400
lunar module's computer for

220
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:32,250
every task.

221
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:34,490
For one final short correction,

222
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,170
NASA had another plan.

223
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:37,840
The men would aim by

224
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:38,920
looking at Earth and the

225
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:40,030
Sun through the windows,

226
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:41,850
fire the engine by hand,

227
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:43,120
and stop it at the

228
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:43,830
right second.

229
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:47,400
Earth was still about 175,000

230
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:48,170
miles away.

231
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:49,570
The engine starts.

232
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:51,140
Lovell keeps the spacecraft

233
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:53,180
lined up. Swigert has the

234
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:53,630
seconds.

235
00:05:54,420 --> 00:05:55,450
A few moments later,

236
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:56,830
the engine stops.

237
00:05:57,540 --> 00:05:59,990
The firing lasts 14.8 seconds,

238
00:06:00,480 --> 00:06:01,460
and NASA says it makes

239
00:06:01,480 --> 00:06:02,710
the correction they needed.

240
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:04,500
It is the kind of

241
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,830
scene that sounds invented.

242
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:07,880
A spacecraft is trying to

243
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:08,890
return from the Moon,

244
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:10,100
and one of the useful

245
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:11,700
tools is a watch that

246
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:12,940
needs no battery from the

247
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:13,280
ship.

248
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:14,800
But that is why NASA

249
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:16,280
had tested it and sent

250
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:17,290
it in the first place.

251
00:06:18,620 --> 00:06:19,400
Houston had worked out the

252
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,010
maneuver.

253
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:21,360
The crew still had to

254
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:22,500
do it, in a damaged,

255
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:24,900
freezing spacecraft, without the

256
00:06:24,920 --> 00:06:25,940
computer they would normally

257
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:27,130
have used for the timing.

258
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:28,800
This is where a backup

259
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:30,090
stops sounding boring.

260
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:32,220
The story is sometimes condensed

261
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:34,100
into one line: a watch

262
00:06:34,120 --> 00:06:35,770
saved Apollo 13.

263
00:06:36,380 --> 00:06:36,990
It is catchy.

264
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:38,780
It also misses the people

265
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,290
who made the rescue possible.

266
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:41,920
The watch could measure an

267
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:43,540
interval. It could not calculate

268
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:45,260
the course, hold the spacecraft

269
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:45,890
steady

270
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:47,530
or make the engine fire.

271
00:06:49,460 --> 00:06:50,200
That was the work of

272
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:51,140
the crew and the teams

273
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:51,770
in Houston.

274
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:53,620
A good backup does not

275
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:55,140
replace them. It gives them

276
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:57,080
one more dependable tool when

277
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:58,710
the normal plan has failed.

278
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:00,620
And the most surprising part

279
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:01,800
is that the watch had

280
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:02,910
been there all along.

281
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:04,440
It did not arrive as

282
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:05,230
a rescue device.

283
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:06,660
It was standard equipment,

284
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:09,080
chosen years before anyone knew

285
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:10,340
what would happen to this

286
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:10,730
crew.

287
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:13,320
Apollo 13 never landed on

288
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:13,770
the Moon.

289
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:15,880
But Lovell, Haise and Swigert

290
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:16,570
came home.

291
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:18,900
NASA later called it a

292
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:20,090
successful failure:

293
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:21,710
the landing failed;

294
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:23,470
the rescue succeeded.

295
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:25,540
The Speedmaster already had its

296
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:26,330
Moon story.

297
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:28,160
Armstrong had left his inside

298
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:29,600
the lander to back up

299
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:30,510
a troublesome timer;

300
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:32,280
Aldrin took his onto the

301
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:32,730
surface.

302
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:34,900
Apollo 13 gave the watch

303
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:36,270
